---
title: Korean Sauerkraut (Kimchi)
servings: 16
time: 3 days
tags: fermented, korean, condiment, side-dish
cuisine: Korean
course: side
source: Nourishing Traditions by Sally Fallon, p. 94 (001.jpg)
---

In a #large bowl{}, place @Napa cabbage{1%head}(cored and shredded), @green onions{1%bunch}(chopped), @carrots{1%cup}(grated), optional @daikon radish{1/2%cup}(grated), @fresh ginger{1%tbsp}(freshly grated), @garlic{3%cloves}(peeled and minced), @dried chile flakes{1/2%tsp}, @sea salt{1%tbsp}, and @whey{4%tbsp}(or 1 extra tbsp sea salt).

Pound the mixture thoroughly with a #wooden pounder{} or #meat hammer{} to release juices.

Pack into a #quart-sized wide-mouth mason jar{2} and press down firmly with the pounder until the juices rise to cover the cabbage, leaving at least 1 inch of headspace below the rim.

Cover tightly and keep at room temperature for about ~{3%days} before transferring to cold storage.
